1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
E → E + T | T. Expanding E never consumes a character: E ⇒ E + T ⇒ E + T + T ⇒ …

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The stack grows by one + T at every step and the input pointer stays at position 1.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The second failure is a shared prefix: S → if E then S | if E then S else S.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Two transformations answer these two failures. Neither belongs to the LL(1) construction; both run before it.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
A → Aα | β says: a β, then any number of α. The helper A′ → αA′ | ε is that same sentence written as a grammar.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
A → βA′ generates β once; then A′ repeats α zero or more times.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
E → E + T | T becomes E → T E′, E′ → + T E′ | ε.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The recursion now sits on the right of the rule, so every expansion consumes a token before it recurses.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Count first: m recursive alternatives and n base alternatives become n + m + 1 productions.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
E has one recursive and one base alternative: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 rules for E. With T the grammar goes from three to four.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
S → Sa | Sb | c | d becomes five: two for S, three for S′.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So a question that asks for the number of rules is answered by the count, before the grammar is written out.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Delete the ε from E′ → + T E′ | ε and ask what E generates now.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Every derivation has to end with E′ ⇒ ε. With no ε there is no ending, so nothing is generated at all.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The enumeration agrees: four strings up to length seven before the deletion, none after.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
ε is the base case of the repetition. It is the one production students delete when they tidy up.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
E → E − T | T parses a − b − c as (a − b) − c. The left recursion is what puts the operator on the left.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
After the rewrite, E → T E′, E′ → − T E′ | ε, the tree leans right: a − (b − c).

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
With a = 10, b = 3 and c = 2 the left tree gives 5 and the right tree gives 9.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Same language, different tree. In a syntax-directed question the tree is what is being marked.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
S → Aa | b and A → Ac | Sd | ε. No rule of S begins with S.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
But S ⇒ Aa ⇒ Sda. The recursion is real, one step longer than the test looks.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The left-corner relation finds it: S reaches A, A reaches S, so S reaches itself.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Both S and A are left recursive; the one-step test finds only A.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
GATE CSE 1998 Q6b asks for an equivalent grammar with no left recursion and the fewest rules.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Order the nonterminals S then A. Substitute S's right sides into A wherever a rule of A begins with S.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
A → Ac | Aad | bd | ε now begins with itself, so the direct rule applies.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The answer: S → Aa | b; A → bdA′ | A′; A′ → cA′ | adA′ | ε. Seven rules, and the language is unchanged.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A → BAa | c with B → ε | b. No rule of A begins with A, so the one-step test says no.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
But B derives ε, so A ⇒ BAa ⇒ Aa. The recursion hides behind a nullable symbol.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Substitute B's right sides into A: A → Aa | bAa | c. Now the recursion is immediate.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Then the direct rule applies: A → bAaA′ | cA′, A′ → aA′ | ε.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A → abc | abd | ae. The first character decides nothing: all three alternatives begin with a.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Factor the longest common prefix: A → abA′, A′ → c | d, and keep A → ae.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
One pass is not enough: A still has ae and abA′, which both begin with a. Factor again.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Two passes turn three productions into five: A → aA″, A″ → e | bA′, A′ → c | d.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Factor the dangling else: S → iEtSS′ | a, S′ → eS | ε. The shared prefix is gone.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
The table still has two entries in M[S′, e]: S′ → eS and S′ → ε. So it is still not LL(1).

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
GATE CSE 2003 Q16: does removing left recursion and factoring suffice for an arbitrary CFG? No.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
S → A | B, A → a, B → a has neither defect and still conflicts on a. Necessary, not sufficient.

